"""
02_Preprocessing.py - EEG preprocessing for microstate analysis
================================================================

Dataset : Dortmund (ds005385), eyes-closed resting state (acq-pre and acq-post)
Input   : the raw EDF recordings
Output  : one preprocessed .fif file per recording (the full, continuous
          recording). The bad segments marked in the annotation CSV files
          (00_annotate_dortmund_data.py) are removed in a separate script.


BACKGROUND: WHAT ARE EEG MICROSTATES?
-------------------------------------
At every time point, the voltages of all electrodes together form a "map" of
the electric field on the scalp (a topography). In resting-state EEG this map
does not change smoothly: it stays almost the same for roughly 60-120 ms and
then quickly switches to a different map. These short, stable periods are
called microstates. Most of the EEG can be described by only 4-7 typical maps,
which are usually named A, B, C, D, ...

The microstate analysis itself (the next script) will:
    1. find the time points where the signal is strongest, i.e. the peaks of
       the Global Field Power (GFP = standard deviation across all electrodes
       at one time point),
    2. group the maps at these peaks into K typical maps with the modified
       k-means algorithm (pycrostates.cluster.ModKMeans),
    3. label every time point of the EEG with its most similar typical map
       ("backfitting") and compute metrics such as the mean duration,
       occurrence and coverage of every microstate.


WHAT IS PREPROCESSING AND WHY DO WE NEED IT?
--------------------------------------------
A raw EEG recording also contains signals that do not come from the brain:
slow drifts (sweat, electrode drift), muscle activity, power line noise
(50 Hz), and movement or eye artifacts. Preprocessing removes or reduces these
signals, so that the microstate maps describe brain activity and not noise.
This script performs the following steps for every recording:

    STEP 1  Load the raw EDF file
    STEP 2  Remove the non-EEG 'Status' channel
    STEP 3  Attach the electrode positions (montage)
    STEP 4  Band-pass filter from 1 to 30 Hz
    STEP 5  Downsample from 1000 Hz to 250 Hz
    STEP 6  Re-reference to the common average
    STEP 7  Save the preprocessed recording as .fif

The recording is not cut here. Filtering, resampling and re-referencing do
not shift the time axis, so the onsets in the annotation CSV files (seconds
from the start of the recording) still match the saved .fif files.


NOT DONE YET: BAD CHANNELS
--------------------------
The annotation CSV files only mark bad time segments, not bad channels. In a
test on four recordings, single noisy channels (4-7 times the median
amplitude) produced microstate maps with a "hot spot" on one electrode only.
The average reference (STEP 6) also spreads the noise of a bad channel to all
other channels. Possible next steps: mark bad channels by hand and
interpolate them (raw.interpolate_bads(), which uses the montage of STEP 3),
ICA (removes eye and muscle activity), or pycrostates' apply_spatial_filter.


SOURCES
-------
Steps 2-6 follow the preprocessing used in the two resources for this task:
    - pycrostates tutorials (keep only EEG channels, then apply the average
      reference before any microstate computation):
      https://pycrostates.readthedocs.io/en/latest/generated/auto_tutorials/
    - Frederic von Wegner's microstate tutorial (set_montage("standard_1005"),
      pick("eeg"), filter(1, 30), set_eeg_reference("average")):
      https://github.com/Frederic-vW/microstates_tutorial/tree/main/python
"""

import mne
import os
import glob
import warnings

# Suppress warning messages from MNE and pandas so the processing output stays
# focused on the status messages printed below.
warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')

from src.config import BASE_DIR_DORT, DIR_PREPDATA


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PARAMETERS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Band-pass filter edges in Hz. Activity below 1 Hz is mostly slow drift
# (sweating, electrode drift) and activity above 30 Hz is mostly muscle
# activity and line noise. Eyes-closed resting EEG is dominated by the alpha
# rhythm (8-12 Hz), which lies well inside this band. 1-30 Hz is the band used
# in Frederic von Wegner's tutorial and planned in 01_unify_eeg_format.py.
# Note: TEST - Removing_Bad_Markings.py uses 0.5-40 Hz for general cleaning;
# that band is not used here.
L_FREQ = 1.0
H_FREQ = 30.0

# New sampling rate in Hz. The Dortmund data are recorded at 1000 Hz (one
# sample every 1 ms). Microstates last about 60-120 ms, so 250 Hz (one sample
# every 4 ms) is still precise enough, while the files become four times
# smaller and the clustering four times faster. 250 Hz can represent
# frequencies up to 125 Hz (the Nyquist frequency = half the sampling rate),
# far above the 30 Hz that remain after filtering, so no information is lost.
# All recordings must have the same sampling rate, because some pycrostates
# settings (for example the smoothing window of the backfitting) are given in
# samples, not in seconds.
TARGET_SFREQ = 250.0

# Name of the standard electrode layout. MNE uses it to look up the 3D position
# of every electrode name (e.g. 'Fz', 'Cz'). All 64 Dortmund channel names
# exist in this layout.
MONTAGE_NAME = "standard_1005"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PATHS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Local setting for Mert's computer (the same path as in the TEST scripts):
# the raw data are on the external E: drive. Comment out this line to use the
# project default.
BASE_DIR_DORT = r"E:\project-healthyageing\02_data\00_download\ds005385-1.0.2"

# Output folder inside 02_data/01_prepdata. Its name lists the settings used,
# so that files made with different settings are never mixed up.
output_dir = DIR_PREPDATA / (
    f"02_microstate_prep_{L_FREQ:g}-{H_FREQ:g}Hz_"
    f"{TARGET_SFREQ:g}Hz_avgref_fif"
)
output_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FIND THE RECORDINGS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Search recursively for pre- and post-acquisition EyesClosed EDF files under
# each subject's EEG folder.
search_pattern = os.path.join(
    BASE_DIR_DORT,
    "sub-*",
    "**",
    "eeg",
    "*task-EyesClosed_acq-*_eeg.edf",
)
edf_files = sorted(glob.glob(search_pattern, recursive=True))

print(f"\nFound {len(edf_files)} EDF files.")

# Stop with a useful message when the data folder is missing or empty.
if not edf_files:
    raise FileNotFoundError(
        f"No EyesClosed EDF files were found below: {BASE_DIR_DORT}"
    )

saved_file_count = 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROCESS EVERY RECORDING
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

for file_path in edf_files:
    file_name = os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(file_path))[0]

    print(f"\nProcessing: {file_name}")

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # STEP 1: Load the raw EDF file
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # preload=True reads the EEG samples into memory. This is required for
    # filtering, resampling and re-referencing, which change the data.
    raw = mne.io.read_raw_edf(file_path, preload=True, verbose=False)

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # STEP 2: Remove the non-EEG 'Status' channel
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 'Status' stores trigger codes of the recording system, not brain
    # signals (see Dortmund_Status_Test.py). It must not be part of the
    # microstate maps, so only the 64 EEG channels are kept.
    if 'Status' in raw.ch_names:
        raw.drop_channels(['Status'])
        print("  -> Dropped 'Status' channel.")

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # STEP 3: Attach the electrode positions (montage)
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # The EDF file only stores channel names, not where the electrodes sit
    # on the head. Microstates are maps, so we need the positions to plot
    # the maps as topographies and for any later spatial step (for example
    # interpolating a broken channel). MNE stops with an error here if a
    # channel name is not in the standard layout.
    raw.set_montage(MONTAGE_NAME, verbose=False)
    print(f"  -> Set montage '{MONTAGE_NAME}'.")

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # STEP 4: Band-pass filter (1-30 Hz)
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Filtering removes the slow drifts and the fast muscle and line noise
    # (see L_FREQ and H_FREQ above). The whole continuous recording is
    # filtered before it is cut into clean intervals, because a filter
    # creates distortions at the start and end of the data ("edge effects").
    # This way the edge effects occur only at the start and end of the
    # whole recording, not at both ends of every saved interval.
    raw.filter(l_freq=L_FREQ, h_freq=H_FREQ, fir_design='firwin', verbose=False)
    print(f"  -> Applied {L_FREQ:g} - {H_FREQ:g} Hz band-pass filter.")

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # STEP 5: Downsample (1000 Hz -> 250 Hz)
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Downsampling must come after the low-pass filter: frequencies above
    # the new Nyquist frequency (125 Hz) would otherwise fold back into the
    # data as fake low frequencies ("aliasing"). MNE's resample() also
    # applies its own anti-aliasing filter as an extra safety step.
    original_sfreq = raw.info['sfreq']
    raw.resample(TARGET_SFREQ, verbose=False)
    print(f"  -> Resampled from {original_sfreq:g} Hz to {TARGET_SFREQ:g} Hz.")

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # STEP 6: Re-reference to the common average
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # EEG measures voltage differences between each electrode and a
    # reference electrode, so the maps depend on where the reference is.
    # With the average reference, the mean of all electrodes is subtracted
    # at every time point, so the map no longer depends on one reference
    # electrode. Microstate analysis expects average-referenced data: GFP
    # is defined as the spatial standard deviation of an average-referenced
    # map. Both tutorials apply this step right before the analysis.
    raw.set_eeg_reference('average', verbose=False)
    print("  -> Applied common average reference.")

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # STEP 7: Save the preprocessed recording
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # The full recording is saved without cutting. The marked bad segments
    # are removed later in a separate script; the time axis still starts at
    # 0 s and meas_date is kept, so the CSV onsets match this file.
    output_file = os.path.join(output_dir, f"{file_name}.fif")
    raw.save(output_file, overwrite=True, verbose=False)
    print(f"  -> Saved to: {output_file}")
    saved_file_count += 1

print(f"\nSaved files: {saved_file_count}")
print(f"Output folder: {output_dir}")
